// The `host-processes` loophole, shipped by the OFFICIAL `host-processes` pack —
// an allowlisted read-only view of host processes.  See internal/hostprocesses for
// the daemon and cmd/yolo-ps for the jail-side CLI wrapper, both of which stay
// BAKED: an official pack may name a baked client, so nothing here waits on the
// pack-shipped-binary capability (broker-as-a-pack.md §12).
//
// It was `bundled_loopholes/host-processes/` until 2026-08-18.  It is the sprint's
// PROVING GROUND (broker-as-a-pack.md §12) — the conversion with none of the
// broker's complexity: no relay, no CA, no intercept, no credential file.  Four of
// its five changes shipped earlier; this file is change 4, and change 5 is the
// verification that the pack-shipped subset accepts the manifest UNCHANGED.  It
// does: `publishes: "socket"` was already the declaration, `requires.command_on_path`
// is not path-scoped, and there is no `jail_env`, no bind mount and no `ca_cert` for
// the subset to refuse.  The only edits below are `default_enabled` and this header.
{
  "name": "host-processes",
  "description": "Allowlisted view of host processes (yolo-ps). Visibility is controlled by this loophole's own `visible` setting.",
  "version": 1,
  // THE LOOPHOLE OWNS ITS CONFIG KEYS NOW (docs/design/pack-config-keys.md).
  // These used to be the top-level `host_processes` block in core's own config
  // schema — one of the two loophole names core hardcoded, and the thing that made
  // "convert the loophole to a pack" separation in appearance only.  The old key is
  // now REFUSED, naming this spelling: it was honored through the step that moved
  // the keys and deleted by the step that moved the manifest, which is this one.
  //
  // Two behaviours moved with them, and both are the point rather than side effects:
  //
  //   FROZEN AT LAUNCH.  The daemon used to re-read the raw workspace config on
  //   every request, so an AGENT could widen its own allowlist mid-session with no
  //   relaunch and therefore no config-approval gate.  yolo now resolves these once
  //   and writes them to the file {settings} names, so a change needs a restart
  //   (OQ-K3).
  //
  //   SCOPE IS DECLARED, PER KEY.  Both are `workspace`, which preserves exactly
  //   what these keys have always been — the manifest's old description said
  //   "workspace controls visibility" and that stays true.  It is safe because a
  //   workspace value reaches this host daemon only through the config-change
  //   approval flow (OQ-K2), and it is now SAID rather than assumed: the default
  //   for an undeclared scope is `user`, so a widening key has to ask.
  "settings": {
    "visible": {
      "type": "string_list",
      "scope": "workspace",
      "default": [],
      // Empty means the loophole shows NOTHING, which is why "enabled" here is a
      // daemon with an empty allowlist rather than a view of the host.
      "description": "process comm names yolo-ps may reveal (empty = show nothing)"
    },
    "fields": {
      "type": "string_list",
      "scope": "workspace",
      // The `ps -o` column list.  Declared here rather than left to the daemon's
      // DefaultFields so that `yolo config-dump` and the settings file both show
      // what is actually in force.  The daemon keeps the same list as its fallback
      // for a settings file it cannot read at all.
      "default": ["pid", "comm", "args", "etime", "%cpu", "%mem", "rss"],
      "description": "ps -o columns returned to the jail"
    }
  },
  // OFF, and this is the commit §1.3's table was waiting for
  // (docs/design/loophole-activation.md R1/R4, the `host-processes` row: "own pack,
  // `default_enabled: false`").  The rename commit kept it TRUE deliberately —
  // flipping it before the pack existed would have taken the capability away with
  // nothing but a config key to restore it.  Now the restore is the thing that turns
  // it on: `packs: ["host-processes"]` in your user config.
  //
  // So this loophole has THREE gates, and OQ-A5 keeps all three on purpose because
  // they answer different questions: select the pack (is it installed), enable the
  // loophole (is it running), list the names (what may it show).  Collapsing the
  // first two would mean a non-empty `visible` list SILENTLY STARTING A HOST
  // DAEMON — presence-activation wearing a different hat.
  //
  // Written out rather than omitted even though absent already means false: this is
  // the manifest a reader checks when they wonder why `yolo-ps` stopped working, and
  // a key that is not there answers nothing.  The switch is
  // `"loopholes": {"host-processes": {"enabled": true}}`.
  //
  // R4's subject is HOST ACCESS, and this loophole is nothing BUT host access — a
  // daemon on the real machine reading the real process table.  Selecting a pack
  // whose entire content is that daemon is not, on its own, the request to run it.
  "default_enabled": false,
  // ``ps`` is a POSIX staple on any host that can run yolo-jail, but
  // gating here keeps us honest — if something pathological has it
  // missing, the loophole goes inactive rather than crashing.
  "requires": {
    "command_on_path": "ps"
  },
  "transport": "loopback-tls",
  "lifecycle": "spawned",
  // No TLS intercept: jails reach the daemon via the ``yolo-ps``
  // client, which reads the endpoint file named by
  // YOLO_SERVICE_HOST_PROCESSES_ENDPOINT and then dials the pinned,
  // token-authenticated loopback listener the file describes.  There is
  // no token env var: the token lives in that file and nowhere else.
  //
  // ``publishes: "socket"`` — the daemon binds a plain AF_UNIX socket at
  // the path yolo substitutes into ``{socket}`` and yolo runs the front
  // over it, so the endpoint file's mode, its key persistence, its
  // constant-time token compare and its length cap are the framework's
  // code rather than this daemon's.  NOTHING JAIL-FACING MOVES: the env
  // var is still YOLO_SERVICE_HOST_PROCESSES_ENDPOINT and the in-jail
  // path is still /run/yolo-services/host-processes.endpoint, because
  // both are functions of the loophole NAME and the transport alone.
  // What moves is upstream and host-only — the socket is bound outside
  // the :rw-mounted services dir, so the retired socket transport stays
  // unreachable from inside the jail.
  //
  // ``preamble`` is left at its manifest default (ON), which is the
  // point of the flip: every connection yolo splices to that socket
  // carries the framework's connection preamble, and
  // hostservice.ServeFrontedUnix reads it — so the tier-1 crossing
  // record and the tier-2 access line name the same jail BY
  // CONSTRUCTION rather than by whatever the client claimed
  // (broker-as-a-pack.md §5.5).
  //
  // ``request_end`` is likewise left at its default ("framed"), and that
  // is not an oversight: hostservice reads exactly one 4+N-byte frame
  // and never to EOF, so "eof" would half-close the upstream for no
  // benefit.
  //
  // ``{settings}`` is the third token yolo substitutes, alongside
  // ``{loophole_dir}`` and ``{state}``, and it resolves to the file yolo WRITES
  // from the ``settings`` declarations above after validating the user's values.
  // It is a PATH and not an env map on purpose: ``loopholes.<name>.env`` is
  // user-scope-only precisely because it reaches a host daemon's spawn
  // environment, and a path is the one thing a spawn may carry.  The workspace
  // supplies VALUES; yolo decides what the file says.
  "host_daemon": {
    "cmd": [
      "yolo", "internal", "daemon", "host-processes",
      "--socket", "{socket}",
      "--settings", "{settings}"
    ],
    "publishes": "socket"
  },
  // The doctor reads the SAME file the daemon does.  It used to hunt for a
  // yolo-jail.jsonc of its own via $YOLO_HOST_PROCESSES_CONFIG or the cwd, so the
  // doctor's answer and the daemon's behaviour came from two different searches and
  // could disagree about which file was in force.
  "doctor_cmd": [
    "yolo", "internal", "daemon", "host-processes", "--self-check",
    "--settings", "{settings}"
  ]
}
